{"id":"h205","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"How much area, in square meters, will one liter of paint cover if it is brushed out to a uniform thickness of 100 microns?\nIs the correct answer \"25 m^2\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct answer is 10 m², not 25 m².","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"One liter equals 0.001 m³.","confidence":0.99,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5551,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"A thickness of 100 microns equals 10⁻⁴ m.","confidence":0.99,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5551,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Area equals volume divided by thickness, giving 10 m² for the stated conditions.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Getting 25 m² would require a film about 40 microns thick.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"25 m² could arise if the wet film were thinner than stated, but this does not match the question.","confidence":0.15,"lean":1,"theta":0.2356,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The question specifies uniform 100-micron thickness for the full liter, so the direct volume-over-thickness calculation applies.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"If 100 microns were dry film thickness, real coverage would be lower (around 4 m² at 40% volume solids), which still does not give 25 m².","confidence":0.8,"lean":-1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i"